1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:05,888
In 2005, a spacecraft circling Saturn noticed

2
00:00:05,888 --> 00:00:09,083
something odd about one of its smallest

3
00:00:09,083 --> 00:00:09,575
moons.

4
00:00:11,025 --> 00:00:14,413
Something there was pushing back against Saturn's

5
00:00:14,413 --> 00:00:15,450
magnetic field.

6
00:00:19,025 --> 00:00:21,911
Months later, Cassini flew in for a

7
00:00:21,911 --> 00:00:22,900
closer look.

8
00:00:27,025 --> 00:00:28,287
And it saw this:

9
00:00:28,287 --> 00:00:31,207
water, spraying out of a frozen moon,

10
00:00:31,207 --> 00:00:32,075
into space.

11
00:00:35,025 --> 00:00:37,475
This is the real image.

12
00:00:41,525 --> 00:00:45,172
A moon barely five hundred kilometres across,

13
00:00:45,172 --> 00:00:46,550
leaking an ocean.

14
00:00:48,025 --> 00:00:50,535
Today we follow that water down,

15
00:00:50,535 --> 00:00:52,575
to the ocean it came from.

16
00:00:56,025 --> 00:00:58,766
And we ask the question scientists now

17
00:00:58,766 --> 00:00:59,703
ask out loud:

18
00:00:59,703 --> 00:01:02,300
could something be alive down there?

19
00:01:04,025 --> 00:01:07,650
Enceladus is one of Saturn's smaller moons.

20
00:01:12,025 --> 00:01:14,586
Small worlds lose their heat fast.

21
00:01:14,586 --> 00:01:15,942
They freeze solid,

22
00:01:15,942 --> 00:01:17,675
and they stay that way.

23
00:01:20,025 --> 00:01:23,025
When Voyager 2 flew past in 1981,

24
00:01:23,025 --> 00:01:26,025
it saw something that didn't fit.

25
00:01:27,475 --> 00:01:29,477
Whole regions were smooth,

26
00:01:29,477 --> 00:01:31,249
with no craters at all.

27
00:01:31,249 --> 00:01:34,175
Something had been repaving this moon.

28
00:01:35,625 --> 00:01:37,683
Fresh ice. Long fractures.

29
00:01:37,683 --> 00:01:39,900
A surface that looked young.

30
00:01:42,125 --> 00:01:44,159
Voyager only had a glimpse.

31
00:01:44,159 --> 00:01:46,570
The answer had to wait more than

32
00:01:46,570 --> 00:01:47,550
twenty years.

33
00:01:50,125 --> 00:01:53,500
Cassini reached Saturn in 2004.

34
00:01:58,125 --> 00:01:59,544
In February 2005,

35
00:01:59,544 --> 00:02:03,299
its magnetometer felt Saturn's magnetic field

36
00:02:03,299 --> 00:02:05,386
bending around Enceladus,

37
00:02:05,386 --> 00:02:08,557
as if something like an atmosphere was

38
00:02:08,557 --> 00:02:09,475
in the way.

39
00:02:10,925 --> 00:02:13,445
In July, Cassini found a cloud of

40
00:02:13,445 --> 00:02:16,575
water vapour hanging over the south pole.

41
00:02:18,025 --> 00:02:19,811
Below it were four long,

42
00:02:19,811 --> 00:02:21,746
warm fractures in the ice.

43
00:02:21,746 --> 00:02:24,500
Scientists called them tiger stripes.

44
00:02:25,950 --> 00:02:28,341
Each one is about a hundred and

45
00:02:28,341 --> 00:02:30,500
thirty-five kilometres long.

46
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:35,100
Over the years,

47
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:38,319
Cassini counted a hundred and one separate

48
00:02:38,319 --> 00:02:39,775
geysers along them.

49
00:02:41,225 --> 00:02:43,599
And the south pole was not just

50
00:02:43,599 --> 00:02:45,208
warm for an ice moon.

51
00:02:45,208 --> 00:02:46,050
It was hot.

52
00:02:49,225 --> 00:02:51,929
It gives off about sixteen gigawatts of

53
00:02:51,929 --> 00:02:54,217
heat, around two and a half times

54
00:02:54,217 --> 00:02:56,435
the power of all the hot springs

55
00:02:56,435 --> 00:02:57,475
in Yellowstone.

56
00:02:58,925 --> 00:03:01,338
The spray was not a thin mist.

57
00:03:01,338 --> 00:03:03,912
It was feeding an entire ring of

58
00:03:03,912 --> 00:03:04,475
Saturn.

59
00:03:05,925 --> 00:03:08,658
Saturn's faint outer E ring is made

60
00:03:08,658 --> 00:03:11,000
mostly of ice from these jets.

61
00:03:12,450 --> 00:03:15,022
So where was all that water coming

62
00:03:15,022 --> 00:03:15,400
from?

63
00:03:20,450 --> 00:03:23,536
Ice can turn straight into vapour without

64
00:03:23,536 --> 00:03:27,073
ever melting. Scientists needed proof of liquid

65
00:03:27,073 --> 00:03:27,525
water.

66
00:03:28,975 --> 00:03:31,000
The proof was salt.

67
00:03:36,975 --> 00:03:40,391
In 2009, Cassini found sodium salts in

68
00:03:40,391 --> 00:03:43,628
ice grains from Saturn's outer ring,

69
00:03:43,628 --> 00:03:45,875
the ring Enceladus feeds.

70
00:03:47,325 --> 00:03:50,081
Salt like that dissolves out of rock,

71
00:03:50,081 --> 00:03:51,272
in liquid water.

72
00:03:51,272 --> 00:03:54,400
Somewhere below, water was touching stone.

73
00:03:55,850 --> 00:03:59,229
By 2014, gravity measurements pointed to a

74
00:03:59,229 --> 00:04:01,240
sea under the south pole,

75
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:03,332
about ten kilometres deep,

76
00:04:03,332 --> 00:04:06,550
under thirty to forty kilometres of ice.

77
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,092
Then came a stranger clue.

78
00:04:10,092 --> 00:04:13,150
Enceladus wobbles as it orbits Saturn.

79
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,116
Scientists tracked craters across hundreds

80
00:04:19,116 --> 00:04:20,452
of Cassini images,

81
00:04:20,452 --> 00:04:22,900
taken over more than seven years.

82
00:04:24,350 --> 00:04:26,581
The wobble was too large for a

83
00:04:26,581 --> 00:04:27,920
moon frozen solid.

84
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:30,375
The ice shell had to be floating.

85
00:04:32,350 --> 00:04:34,604
In 2015 came the verdict:

86
00:04:34,604 --> 00:04:35,956
a global ocean,

87
00:04:35,956 --> 00:04:38,750
wrapped around the entire moon.

88
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:41,372
A hidden ocean,

89
00:04:41,372 --> 00:04:44,028
on a moon with roughly the surface

90
00:04:44,028 --> 00:04:45,200
area of Turkey.

91
00:04:46,700 --> 00:04:48,902
Life as we know it needs three

92
00:04:48,902 --> 00:04:50,444
things: liquid water,

93
00:04:50,444 --> 00:04:51,838
a source of energy,

94
00:04:51,838 --> 00:04:53,600
and the right chemistry.

95
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:57,900
Water, Enceladus clearly has.

96
00:05:03,050 --> 00:05:06,488
In 2008, Cassini flew straight through the

97
00:05:06,488 --> 00:05:08,125
plume and tasted it.

98
00:05:09,575 --> 00:05:12,197
It was full of organic molecules,

99
00:05:12,197 --> 00:05:15,375
about twenty times denser than expected.

100
00:05:17,575 --> 00:05:20,912
Scientists compared it to carbonated water

101
00:05:20,912 --> 00:05:23,375
with an essence of natural gas.

102
00:05:25,575 --> 00:05:28,827
In 2015, tiny grains of silica pointed

103
00:05:28,827 --> 00:05:30,367
to something more:

104
00:05:30,367 --> 00:05:32,849
hot water reacting with rock,

105
00:05:32,849 --> 00:05:34,475
on the ocean floor.

106
00:05:35,925 --> 00:05:38,853
Then, on the 28th of October 2015,

107
00:05:38,853 --> 00:05:42,383
Cassini made its deepest dive through the

108
00:05:42,383 --> 00:05:42,900
plume.

109
00:05:44,350 --> 00:05:45,872
It found hydrogen,

110
00:05:45,872 --> 00:05:48,325
about one percent of the gas.

111
00:05:52,350 --> 00:05:55,499
Hydrogen like that forms when hot water

112
00:05:55,499 --> 00:05:56,872
reacts with rock.

113
00:05:56,872 --> 00:05:58,245
And for microbes,

114
00:05:58,245 --> 00:05:59,133
it is food:

115
00:05:59,133 --> 00:06:00,425
chemical energy.

116
00:06:01,875 --> 00:06:04,324
In 2023, one more ingredient:

117
00:06:04,324 --> 00:06:07,785
phosphates, at least a hundred times more

118
00:06:07,785 --> 00:06:10,825
concentrated than in Earth's oceans.

119
00:06:12,275 --> 00:06:15,862
Phosphorus forms the backbone of every DNA

120
00:06:15,862 --> 00:06:17,400
molecule on Earth.

121
00:06:20,275 --> 00:06:22,377
Water. Energy. Chemistry.

122
00:06:22,377 --> 00:06:24,900
Every box we know how to tick.

123
00:06:28,275 --> 00:06:30,771
To see what that chemistry can do,

124
00:06:30,771 --> 00:06:32,900
we only have to look at home.

125
00:06:34,775 --> 00:06:37,278
Two and a half kilometres below the

126
00:06:37,278 --> 00:06:39,925
Pacific, there is no sunlight at all.

127
00:06:42,375 --> 00:06:46,036
In 1977, scientists exploring the Galápagos

128
00:06:46,036 --> 00:06:49,356
Rift found hot springs on the seafloor,

129
00:06:49,356 --> 00:06:51,825
and life crowded around them.

130
00:06:53,275 --> 00:06:55,295
Clams, crabs, tubeworms,

131
00:06:55,295 --> 00:06:57,400
living in total darkness.

132
00:06:59,775 --> 00:07:01,875
At the base of that food web

133
00:07:01,875 --> 00:07:04,575
are microbes that live on chemistry,

134
00:07:04,575 --> 00:07:05,550
not sunlight.

135
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,484
At places like the Lost City vent

136
00:07:09,484 --> 00:07:11,140
field in the Atlantic,

137
00:07:11,140 --> 00:07:13,775
hydrogen from the rocks feeds them.

138
00:07:15,225 --> 00:07:17,474
Hydrogen. Carbon dioxide.

139
00:07:17,474 --> 00:07:19,632
Warm rock. Liquid water.

140
00:07:19,632 --> 00:07:23,500
The same recipe Cassini found at Enceladus.

141
00:07:24,950 --> 00:07:28,047
Some scientists think life on Earth itself

142
00:07:28,047 --> 00:07:30,775
may have started at vents like these.

143
00:07:32,950 --> 00:07:36,564
Cassini was built before anyone knew Enceladus

144
00:07:36,564 --> 00:07:37,586
had an ocean.

145
00:07:37,586 --> 00:07:39,550
It could not detect life.

146
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,430
On the 15th of September 2017,

147
00:07:43,430 --> 00:07:46,508
it was steered into Saturn on purpose,

148
00:07:46,508 --> 00:07:49,586
so it could never crash into Enceladus

149
00:07:49,586 --> 00:07:51,125
and contaminate it.

150
00:07:52,575 --> 00:07:54,793
But the jets are still going.

151
00:07:54,793 --> 00:07:57,776
In 2023, the James Webb Space Telescope

152
00:07:57,776 --> 00:08:01,217
showed the plume reaching nearly ten thousand

153
00:08:01,217 --> 00:08:02,900
kilometres into space.

154
00:08:04,350 --> 00:08:07,133
That is about nineteen times the width

155
00:08:07,133 --> 00:08:08,525
of the moon itself.

156
00:08:12,350 --> 00:08:15,825
In 2024, the European Space Agency named

157
00:08:15,825 --> 00:08:19,039
Enceladus the top target for its next

158
00:08:19,039 --> 00:08:20,950
large science mission.

159
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:25,404
The idea: land near the tiger stripes

160
00:08:25,404 --> 00:08:27,921
and catch the ocean as it falls

161
00:08:27,921 --> 00:08:30,438
back down. The earliest launch:

162
00:08:30,438 --> 00:08:31,250
the 2040s.

163
00:08:32,700 --> 00:08:35,434
So, is something alive in the ocean

164
00:08:35,434 --> 00:08:36,450
of Enceladus?

165
00:08:40,700 --> 00:08:43,200
Nobody knows. Not yet.

166
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:50,291
But for the first time,

167
00:08:50,291 --> 00:08:52,228
we know a place in our solar

168
00:08:52,228 --> 00:08:54,925
system where every ingredient is there,

169
00:08:56,700 --> 00:08:59,145
and the ocean sends us samples for

170
00:08:59,145 --> 00:09:01,159
free. We just have to go and

171
00:09:01,159 --> 00:09:01,950
catch them.
